      "id": 1,
      "text": "La Porte became the oldest incorporated town in east Harris County when it was officially incorporated on August 10, 1892.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        13
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.laportetx.gov/589/History-of-La-Porte",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The La Porte Historic Depot Museum, housed in the former Southern Pacific Railroad depot, displays photos and artifacts that capture the city’s early tourist heyday.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        3,
        28
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia",
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/La_Porte%2C_Texas",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "In the 1920s and 1930s, Sylvan Beach Amusement Park turned La Porte into a nationally recognized destination with beauty contests and famous band performances.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        10
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/La_Porte%2C_Texas",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Established in 1909, the Historic La Porte Colored School now operates as a museum that honors Black history and education in the community.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        20
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.visitlaportetx.com/history",
      "category": "people",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "La Porte earned the nickname “Coney Island of the South” while it was a major tourist spot and a regular stop on Houston’s Moonlight Express train.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        22
